WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:03.962
A big family lunch.

2
00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:10.491
The cook is making dumplings, two hundred of them, one at a time, and a diner eats them as they come.

3
00:00:10.667 --> 00:00:14.707
Then the diner's phone rings, with dumpling 1 still on the plate.

4
00:00:14.707 --> 00:00:16.945
Somebody's aunt, by the sound of it.

5
00:00:16.945 --> 00:00:22.228
In a plain flow, the cook stands at the table holding dumpling 2 until the call ends.

6
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.703
Put a shelf in the hatch, and she can keep cooking while the diner talks.

7
00:00:26.703 --> 00:00:29.709
In code, that's buffer(10): a shelf with ten spots.

8
00:00:29.867 --> 00:00:32.714
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:32.714 --> 00:00:34.571
How far does she actually get?

10
00:00:34.571 --> 00:00:38.719
While the diner is on the phone, how many of her emit calls return?

11
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:42.209
Eleven.

12
00:00:42.209 --> 00:00:44.385
Ten dumplings wait on the shelf.

13
00:00:44.385 --> 00:00:49.075
And one more, dumpling 1, left the shelf when the diner picked it up.

14
00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:50.571
Ten, plus one in hand.

15
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:51.990
Here's the machine.

16
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:54.700
It's the wall with a hatch from lesson 3.

17
00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:01.643
buffer moves the cook into a second coroutine, and her emit becomes a send into a channel with ten spots.

18
00:01:01.643 --> 00:01:04.221
send returns as long as a spot is free.

19
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:10.724
On our side, a loop takes each dumpling off the shelf, and calls the real emit, which runs the diner.

20
00:01:10.724 --> 00:01:13.730
flowOn builds this machine to change the thread.

21
00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.047
buffer builds it to change the shelf.

22
00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:20.568
So the count is always one more than the shelf.

23
00:01:20.568 --> 00:01:24.049
No buffer: zero, because emit runs the diner.

24
00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:28.302
buffer(0), a hand-over where both hands must meet: one.

25
00:01:28.302 --> 00:01:29.772
buffer(10): eleven.

26
00:01:29.772 --> 00:01:33.329
And buffer() with no size has 64 spots, so 65.

27
00:01:33.467 --> 00:01:36.502
Even one dumpling of slack matters.

28
00:01:36.502 --> 00:01:42.227
buffer(0) turns lesson 1's dinner, 1,200 milliseconds, into 1,000.

29
00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:45.744
So far, a full shelf made the cook wait.

30
00:01:45.744 --> 00:01:49.987
That's the default overflow policy, SUSPEND, and nothing is lost.

31
00:01:49.987 --> 00:01:52.206
The other two policies never wait.

32
00:01:52.206 --> 00:01:55.469
When the shelf is full, something goes in the bin.

33
00:01:55.667 --> 00:02:05.704
Dumplings are the wrong food for the bin, so take something where only the newest value matters: the oven's thermometer, and the little screen on the oven door.

34
00:02:05.704 --> 00:02:09.781
The screen is stuck drawing reading 1, while 200 readings arrive.

35
00:02:09.933 --> 00:02:14.479
buffer(10) shows all 200, because the thermometer waited.

36
00:02:14.479 --> 00:02:20.381
DROP_OLDEST bins the oldest waiting reading, and shows 1, then 191 to 200.

37
00:02:20.381 --> 00:02:24.289
DROP_LATEST bins each new one, and shows 1 to 11.

38
00:02:24.467 --> 00:02:31.672
And conflate keeps only the newest: one spot, where each new reading pushes the waiting one into the bin.

39
00:02:31.672 --> 00:02:33.936
The screen shows 1, and then 200.

40
00:02:33.936 --> 00:02:35.857
It never makes anybody wait.

41
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.187
Here's the trap.

42
00:02:37.187 --> 00:02:42.973
A helper wants the newest 64, and writes buffer with DROP_OLDEST, and no size.

43
00:02:42.973 --> 00:02:46.830
With a drop policy, the default size means one spot.

44
00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.094
So that line is conflate under another name.

45
00:02:50.094 --> 00:02:51.948
If you mean 64, write 64.

46
00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:57.125
So before you pick a drop policy, ask what one lost value costs.

47
00:02:57.125 --> 00:02:59.586
A stale temperature is worth nothing.

48
00:02:59.586 --> 00:03:02.980
A lost plate of dumplings is a guest with no lunch.

49
00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.776
Plates get SUSPEND, always.

50
00:03:05.667 --> 00:03:08.473
Two shelves side by side don't stay two.

51
00:03:08.473 --> 00:03:14.717
buffer(10) then buffer(20) snap together into one shelf of 30, so the cook gets 31 ahead.

52
00:03:14.717 --> 00:03:16.190
That's called fusion.

53
00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:22.995
And a drop policy at the end relabels the whole shelf: buffer(10) then conflate is just conflate.

54
00:03:23.867 --> 00:03:25.209
Last, collectLatest.

55
00:03:25.209 --> 00:03:28.297
A new reading cancels the drawing in progress.

56
00:03:28.297 --> 00:03:34.606
But a cancelled drawing must wipe its half-drawn number first, and that takes 50 milliseconds.

57
00:03:34.606 --> 00:03:37.895
Readings come every 100, and a drawing takes 300.

58
00:03:37.895 --> 00:03:40.647
When does the thermometer emit reading 3?

59
00:03:43.867 --> 00:03:45.898
Most people say 300.

60
00:03:45.898 --> 00:03:52.093
The virtual clock says 350, and the run ends at 700, not 600.

61
00:03:52.267 --> 00:03:59.026
collectLatest cancels the old block, then waits for it to finish, and it does that inside the thermometer's emit.

62
00:03:59.026 --> 00:04:04.291
So the thermometer stands at the hatch with reading 2, while the screen wipes reading 1.

63
00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:11.577
So use the Latest operators only for work you can throw away, like drawing a number, and keep the cleanup short.

64
00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:13.838
Here it is in code.

65
00:04:13.838 --> 00:04:17.880
Plates get buffer(64): a full shelf makes the cook wait.

66
00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.468
Readings get conflate.

67
00:04:19.468 --> 00:04:27.768
In Java, plates go through a queue with 64 slots, and Reactor's onBackpressureLatest keeps only the newest reading.

68
00:04:28.533 --> 00:04:30.426
So, how far does the cook get?

69
00:04:30.426 --> 00:04:35.914
One more than the shelf holds, and then she waits, unless you chose a policy that bins.

70
00:04:35.914 --> 00:04:40.708
Shelves side by side snap into one, and collectLatest waits for the cleanup.

71
00:04:40.708 --> 00:04:42.853
Pick by what one lost value costs.

72
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:43.861
Quick check.

73
00:04:43.861 --> 00:04:45.869
buffer(10), then buffer(20).

74
00:04:45.869 --> 00:04:48.809
The diner is stuck on the first dumpling.

75
00:04:48.809 --> 00:04:50.387
How many emits return?

76
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:53.828
31.

77
00:04:53.828 --> 00:05:00.056
The two shelves fuse into one shelf of 30, plus one dumpling in the diner's hands.
